$post_body = <<<'HTML'
<p>Draperies are heavier, often lined fabric panels made to block light, add privacy, and insulate a room, while sheers are lightweight, see through panels that soften sunlight without covering the view. At <a href="%BASE%/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Creative Blinds and Drapes</a>, homeowners in the Aurora, Illinois area ask about this difference often because the two products solve different problems in the same room. The short answer: draperies control light and privacy, and sheers control glare and softness.</p>
%IMAGE1%
<h2 id="what-are-draperies-made-for">What Are Draperies Made For?</h2>
<p>Draperies are full length fabric panels, usually lined, that hang from a rod and close across a window. <a href="%BASE%/window-treatments/curtains-and-drapes/draperies/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Custom draperies</a> from Norman Window Fashions are measured and sewn for a specific window, so they hang straight and close flush against the frame.</p>
<p>Common reasons homeowners choose draperies:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Blocking light in a bedroom or media room</li>
  <li>Adding a layer of insulation against cold glass in winter</li>
  <li>Creating a formal look in a dining room or living room</li>
  <li>Pairing with blinds or shades for layered light control</li>
  <li>Covering an oversized or oddly shaped window opening</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="what-makes-sheers-different">What Makes Sheers Different?</h2>
<p>Sheers are the opposite of draperies in weight and purpose. The fabric is thin and semi transparent, so it filters sunlight and keeps a room bright while still softening the view of the street or a neighboring yard. <a href="%BASE%/window-treatments/curtains-and-drapes/sheers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sheers</a> do not block light the way draperies do, and most buyers in Aurora and Naperville choose them for daytime privacy, not nighttime privacy.</p>
<p>Sheers tend to work well for:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Rooms that need daylight but some screening from outside view</li>
  <li>A soft layer hung in front of blinds or behind draperies</li>
  <li>Kitchens, breakfast nooks, and sunrooms</li>
  <li>Homes with a casual, relaxed decorating style</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="draperies-vs-sheers-at-a-glance">Draperies vs Sheers at a Glance</h2>
<div class="compare-table-wrap">
<table class="compare-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Draperies</th><th>Sheers</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Light control</td><td>Blocks most or all light</td><td>Filters and softens light</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Privacy</td><td>Full privacy day and night</td><td>Daytime privacy mainly</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fabric weight</td><td>Heavy, often lined</td><td>Lightweight, semi transparent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Best room fit</td><td>Bedrooms, media rooms, dining rooms</td><td>Sunrooms, kitchens, layered looks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical pairing</td><td>Hung alone or over blinds</td><td>Hung behind draperies or alone</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
%IMAGE2%
<h2 id="can-draperies-and-sheers-be-used-together">Can Draperies and Sheers Be Used Together?</h2>
<p><strong>Many homeowners do not have to choose only one.</strong></p>
<p>Layering a sheer panel behind a drapery panel on the same rod, or on a double rod, gives daytime light control from the sheer and nighttime privacy from the drapery. This layered approach is common in formal living rooms and in bedrooms that face the street.</p>
<p>Reasons to layer both:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Daytime brightness without losing evening privacy</li>
  <li>A softer look than blinds alone</li>
  <li>An added layer of insulation against drafts</li>
  <li>Flexibility to adjust light through the day</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on how homeowners are rethinking window coverings, see our <a href="%BASE%/blog/what-are-people-using-instead-of-blinds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">guide on what people are using instead of blinds</a>.</p>
<p>Draperies and sheers both have a place in a well planned window, and the right choice depends on how much light and privacy a room needs at different times of day. A member of the design team can look at your windows and walk through fabric and lining options in person. To get specific guidance for your home, <a href="%BASE%/contact/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schedule a free in-home consultation</a> with our team.</p>
HTML;

